  "id": "DrugBank_DB01114 - MONDO_7448",
  "verbalization": "Chlorpheniramine, a second-generation antihistamine, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Familial Dermatographia through a series of mechanistic links. The evidence suggests that chlorpheniramine may contribute to the condition via the side effect of hypoplastic anemia or erythroid hypoplasia, which are both associated with Dexchlorpheniramine, a metabolite of chlorpheniramine. These conditions are linked to Familial Dermatographia, indicating a potential role in the pathophysiology of the disease. The shared pathway from chlorpheniramine to Dexchlorpheniramine, and subsequently to Familial Dermatographia, supports a mechanistic connection, though the exact nature of the relationship remains unclear. The evidence is consistent with a possible role of antihistamines in the development of dermatological conditions, but further investigation is needed to establish a direct causal link.",
